nodecode-northstar is a rewrite of mrcoxis-2002's mrcoxis-2002/hermes-northstar:

  https://github.com/mrcoxis-2002/hermes-northstar
  commit 9e4976cb416c65c702698e8da67e24a023a2fc14

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Ryan Cox (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's idea -- a read-only readiness check that reads the
files a session is told to obey and reports where they disagree -- as a
Nodecode cell over this harness's own instruction sources: the home's
AGENTS.md (the operator's rules), SYSTEM.md and SOUL.md, the project's
AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, .cursorrules and README.md in the session's folder and
the folders above it, config.jsonc, and any SKILL.md under the home's
skills/. The three checks are the original's: near-duplicate directives, with
frontmatter and metadata lines left out; contradictions between an allow and
a deny phrase, downgraded to a warning when the two sides name different
scopes; and paths that resolve nowhere, the URL, markdown-link, inline-code,
fenced-code and example contexts skipped. The plugin's two tools became the
northstar_audit tool and the /northstar command, and its text and JSON
reports are the same two formats.

Left out: northstar_status, which answered a static placeholder rather than
what the plugin was enforcing, so the same facts are (northstar:status)
instead; the pre_llm_call hook, which injected the last audit's first three
errors into the first turn of a session from a cache the plugin kept in
memory -- the audit here runs when it is asked, and its report is one command
away rather than riding every session's prompt; and the plugin's own
directories (a Hermes home beside a Python package, its demos and fixtures),
because Nodecode's home and its sessions' folders are where the files
actually are.
